<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="font-size:120%;">1.  Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As large language models (LLMs) continue to reshape the landscape of NLP, they are increasingly applied to complex and ingenious tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schmidgall et al. (2025)</a></cite>. With the scaling of LLMs into super-large trillion-parameter models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fedus et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hudson et al. (2023)</a></cite>, and multi-agent architectures requiring hundreds of API calls <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Islam et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jang et al. (2024)</a></cite>, the computational footprint has increased drastically <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2025)</a></cite>, resulting in high inference latency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wan et al. (2023)</a></cite> and energy consumption <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stojkovic et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To counter the consequences of the scaling law for LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaplan et al. (2020)</a></cite>, several methods were introduced to improve model efficiency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wan et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Prompt compression is one of the subfields where the input prompting tokens are reduced while retaining considerable performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025)</a></cite>. Compression is either achieved on the hard prompts (natural language input text) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sennrich et al. (2016)</a></cite> or the soft prompt (trainable embedding-size vectors)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. (2023a)</a></cite>, with the former being more popular due to its interpretability and compatibility with closed-source models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Current hard prompt compression methods fall short in three key aspects. First, some methods rely on summarizing the input prompt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chuang et al. (2024)</a></cite>, which can inadvertently discard semantically critical tokens. Second, these methods tend to retain low-information tokens, such as articles and conjunctions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2023)</a></cite>. Third, all methods either rely on an auxiliary smaller LLM with billions of parameters or a training paradigm. For instance, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chuang et al. (2024)</a></cite> use a Vicuna 7B model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiang et al. (2023)</a></cite>, which introduces a significant parameter overhead.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We address these challenges by introducing a prompt compression strategy that uses saliency scores generated from state-of-the-art pre-trained token attribution methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Modarressi et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Modarressi et al. (2023)</a></cite>. These saliency scores can capture the semantically rich token while filtering the redundant ones. This is achieved without any training paradigm, with minimal parameter overhead of a single 110M BERT model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al. (2019)</a></cite>, substantially lower than that of existing methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our criteria for filtering are grounded in two linguistic principles underlying textual redundancy. First, there is an intrinsic redundancy to any form of text produced by humans <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wit and Gillette (1999)</a></cite>. For instance, in the text, “What are your plans for the weekend, by the way?”, the phrase “by the way” contributes no additional meaning to an LLM. Second, the English language has low-importance tokens that add little to no semantic weight, generally in the form of articles and auxiliary verbs. For instance, most of the meaning is conveyed if the statement “I have received a parcel.”, is simplified to “I received parcel.", with the latter mimicking simplified English, often used by non-native speakers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shubert et al. (1995)</a></cite>. Our filtering methods eliminate both forms of redundancy.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our key contributions are as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a novel, training-free prompt compression strategy for LLMs that controllably filters low-importance tokens based on saliency scores from pre-trained encoders.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Experiments on four tasks reveal that a 20% prompt reduction retains the performance across most models and tasks with minimal parameter overhead.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We reveal key behavioral insights into the performance scaling of models with the inference cost and possible task contamination in common benchmarks.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="font-size:120%;">2.  Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:110%;">2.1.  LLM Efficiency</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The current literature on LLM efficiency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wan et al. (2023)</a></cite> stems from the literature on efficient transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tay et al. (2022)</a></cite>, but for the autoregressive setting of LLMs. Inference-time efficiency can be achieved via model compression methods, such as quantization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dettmers et al. (2023b)</a></cite>, parameter pruning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al. (2023)</a></cite>, low-rank decomposition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2023a)</a></cite>, and knowledge distillation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Timiryasov and Tastet (2023)</a></cite>. Training efficiency can be achieved during pre-training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al. (2024)</a></cite> and fine-tuning, either through parameter-efficient <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2022)</a></cite> or memory-efficient <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dettmers et al. (2023a)</a></cite> approaches. Finally, architectural changes also improve efficiency, <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, efficient attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Child et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ainslie et al. (2023)</a></cite> and state-space models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu and Dao (2023)</a></cite>. Distinct from these methods, our work adopts a data-centric approach where the input prompt is optimized instead of changing the underlying weights or architecture of the model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:110%;">2.2.  Prompt Compression</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prompt compression aims to minimize input redundancy while retaining task-relevant information. Trainable <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">soft-prompts</span> achieve these through learned embeddings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. (2023b)</a></cite>, while sacrificing the interpretability of the prompt text. In contrast, compressing <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hard prompts</span> preserves interpretability via filtering important tokens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2023b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2023)</a></cite> or paraphrasing the prompt text into a reduced version <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chuang et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Context-heavy tasks, <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, conversation systems, benefit from prompt compression but have focused on sentential redundancy or summarization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Santra et al. (2023)</a></cite>. Our work instead aims at token-level reduction, offering higher granularity without losing semantic content.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While our work primarily filters the top <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\%</annotation></semantics></math> tokens based on the attribution scores, a key distinction is that we use smaller BERT-class text encoders to gain attribution scores. Previous works lack such efficiency as they rely on smaller auto-regressive models, <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2023)</a></cite> uses GPT-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al. (2019)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chuang et al. (2024)</a></cite> uses Vicuna 7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiang et al. (2023)</a></cite>. While the compressor model has fewer parameters than the interference model, its overhead is significantly higher than that of the discriminative token attribution methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:110%;">2.3.  Token Attribution</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The self-attention weights in transformers have been primarily explored as a proxy for token importance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Htut et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clark (2019)</a></cite>, yet they fail to align with the true attribution of tokens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jain and Wallace (2019)</a></cite>. While including vector norms can partially mitigate this issue <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kobayashi et al. (2020)</a></cite>, it overlooks the intricacies of the non-attention components in a transformer. Instead, we rely on subsequent token attribution methods: GlobEnc <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Modarressi et al. (2022)</a></cite>, which quantifies global token attribution by aggregating layer-wise analyses across the entire encoder using attention rollout and vector norms, and DecompX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Modarressi et al. (2023)</a></cite>, which propagates locally decomposed token representations globally to incorporate all model components, including non-linear feed-forward networks. These methods were selected for their superior ability to capture comprehensive semantic contributions beyond raw attention weights, enabling accurate identification of salient tokens with minimal computational overhead.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.16439v1/overview-v2.1.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:659/146;" width="659" height="146" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>
Overview of the <span id="S2.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FrugalPrompt</span> pipeline. The input prompt with tokens <math id="S2.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{1},t_{2},\dots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{1},t_{2},\dots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is passed to our task-specific token attribution module to generate the saliency scores for each token <math id="S2.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle s_{1},s_{2},\dots,s_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle s_{1},s_{2},\dots,s_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. The tokens are ordered based on their saliency in a permutation <math id="S2.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> and the top <math id="S2.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=k\%*n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">%</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=k\%*n</annotation></semantics></math> tokens are filtered while maintaining the order of the text to form the reduced text <math id="S2.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{\pi(1)},t_{\pi(2)},\dots,t_{\pi(p)}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{\pi(1)},t_{\pi(2)},\dots,t_{\pi(p)}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. The reduced text is inferred by a frozen LLM to generate the answer tokens <math id="S2.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle a_{1},a_{2},\dots,a_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle a_{1},a_{2},\dots,a_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="font-size:120%;">3.  Preliminaries: Token Attribution</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To identify which tokens in an input text sequence carry significant influence on the language model’s decision-making process for a given task, we compute the token attribution scores using task-specific BERT-class encoders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al. (2019)</a></cite>. We obtain the saliency scores from these models by adopting two state-of-the-art (SoTA) token attribution methods: the global token attribution analysis method (GlobEnc) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Modarressi et al. (2022)</a></cite> and the globally propagated locally decomposed attribution analysis method (DecompX) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Modarressi et al. (2023)</a></cite>, which are discussed in this section.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:110%;">3.1.  GlobEnc</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GlobEnc aggregates the layerwise analysis method <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{\text{{Enc}}}\coloneqq(\|\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$x$}}}_{i\leftarrow j}\|)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Enc</mtext></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{\text{{Enc}}}\coloneqq(\|\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$x$}}}_{i\leftarrow j}\|)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> from input token <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> to output token <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, across all the encoder layers using attention rollout <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abnar and Zuidema (2020)</a></cite>. As elucidated in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kobayashi et al. (2020)</a></cite>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{i\leftarrow j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{i\leftarrow j}</annotation></semantics></math> approximates the attributed vector contribution from input token <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> to output token <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, computed as the weighted and transformed value vector <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{ij}f(x_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{ij}f(x_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is the affine transformation to value space. The vector is adjusted for residual connections and layer normalization to capture the true <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mixed</span> influence beyond attention weights.
For each encoder layer, the output is computed as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$z$}}}_{i\leftarrow j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$z$}}}_{i\leftarrow j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=g_{z_{i}^{+}}\left(\sum_{h=1}^{H}\alpha_{ij}^{h}f^{h}(x_{i})+\mathbbm{1}[i=j]x_{i}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=g_{z_{i}^{+}}\left(\sum_{h=1}^{H}\alpha_{ij}^{h}f^{h}(x_{i})+\mathbbm{1}[i=j]x_{i}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$x$}}}_{i\leftarrow j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$x$}}}_{i\leftarrow j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx g_{\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$z$}}}_{i}^{+}}(\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$z$}}}_{i\leftarrow j})=\frac{\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$z$}}}_{i\leftarrow j}-\operatorname{mean}(\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$z$}}}_{i\leftarrow j})}{\operatorname{std-dev}(\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$z$}}}_{i}^{+})}\odot\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>std</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>dev</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx g_{\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$z$}}}_{i}^{+}}(\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$z$}}}_{i\leftarrow j})=\frac{\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$z$}}}_{i\leftarrow j}-\operatorname{mean}(\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$z$}}}_{i\leftarrow j})}{\operatorname{std-dev}(\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$z$}}}_{i}^{+})}\odot\gamma</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">such that <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{\text{{ResLN}}}\coloneqq(\|\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$z$}}}_{i\leftarrow j}\|)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">ResLN</mtext></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{\text{{ResLN}}}\coloneqq(\|\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$z$}}}_{i\leftarrow j}\|)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the attribution analysis to measure the influence of an encoder layer’s input token <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> on its corresponding output token <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, considering the residual connections and layer normalizations in the attention block. Here, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{ij}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{ij}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the raw attention weight from the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th token to the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th token in the attention head <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\{1,\dots,H\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\{1,\dots,H\}</annotation></semantics></math>, collectively forming the set of attention weights <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{A}^{h}=\{\alpha_{ij}^{h}\bigm|1\leq i,j\leq n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}^{h}=\{\alpha_{ij}^{h}\bigm|1\leq i,j\leq n\}</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>th attention head and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{h}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{h}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the transformation function as defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kobayashi et al. (2020)</a></cite>. For layer <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, the attention rollout with respect to the input is computed as per the following recursive function:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$\mathbf{A}$}}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$\mathbf{A}$}}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{cases}\hat{\mathbf{A}}_{l}\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$\mathbf{A}$}}}_{l-1}\quad\text{; if}\quad l&gt;1\\
\hat{\mathbf{A}}_{l}\quad\quad\quad\,\text{; if}\quad l=1\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>; if</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mspace style="width:3.17em;" width="3.17em"></mspace><mtext>; if</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{cases}\hat{\mathbf{A}}_{l}\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$\mathbf{A}$}}}_{l-1}\quad\text{; if}\quad l&gt;1\\
\hat{\mathbf{A}}_{l}\quad\quad\quad\,\text{; if}\quad l=1\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{A}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{A}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0.5\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{l}+0.5\mathbf{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0.5\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{l}+0.5\mathbf{I}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is the mean raw attention grid across all the heads relevant to the layer <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:110%;">3.2.  DecompX</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While GlobEnc aggregates layer-wise analyses with a modified attention rollout and approximates FFNs via residuals and scalar norms, it risks information loss. DecompX improves this by propagating the locally decomposed vectors throughout the layers to construct a global decomposition, including FFNs with linear activation approximations, and extending attributions to prediction-based values. Formally, the locally decomposed vector at layer <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> is expressed as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$z$}}}^{\ell}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\tilde{\text{$z$}}}^{\ell}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{LN}\left(\sum_{k=1}^{N}\left[\mathbf{x}^{\ell}_{i\Leftarrow k}+\mathbf{z}^{\ell}_{i\Leftarrow k}\right]\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>LN</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{LN}\left(\sum_{k=1}^{N}\left[\mathbf{x}^{\ell}_{i\Leftarrow k}+\mathbf{z}^{\ell}_{i\Leftarrow k}\right]\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where the normalized contribution is given by:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E6X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{LN}\left(\mathbf{z}_{i}^{+\ell}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>LN</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{LN}\left(\mathbf{z}_{i}^{+\ell}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{k=1}^{N}\underbrace{g_{z^{+\ell}_{i}}\left(\mathbf{z}_{i\Leftarrow k}^{+\ell}\right)}_{\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell}}+\boldsymbol{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></munder></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝜷</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{k=1}^{N}\underbrace{g_{z^{+\ell}_{i}}\left(\mathbf{z}_{i\Leftarrow k}^{+\ell}\right)}_{\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell}}+\boldsymbol{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E6Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle g_{z^{+\ell}_{i}}\left(\mathbf{z}_{i\Leftarrow k}^{+\ell}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle g_{z^{+\ell}_{i}}\left(\mathbf{z}_{i\Leftarrow k}^{+\ell}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\frac{\mathbf{z}_{i\Leftarrow k}^{+\ell}-m\left(\mathbf{z}_{i\Leftarrow k}^{+\ell}\right)}{s\left(\mathbf{z}_{i}^{+\ell}\right)}\odot\boldsymbol{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>𝜸</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\frac{\mathbf{z}_{i\Leftarrow k}^{+\ell}-m\left(\mathbf{z}_{i\Leftarrow k}^{+\ell}\right)}{s\left(\mathbf{z}_{i}^{+\ell}\right)}\odot\boldsymbol{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">DecompX explicitly integrates all encoder layer components, notably the non-linear feed-forward networks, to enhance representational fidelity. The <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">FFN</span> operation is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E7X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{z}_{\text{FFN}}^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mtext>FFN</mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{z}_{\text{FFN}}^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{FFN}(\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{i}^{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>FFN</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{FFN}(\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{i}^{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E7Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=f_{\text{act}}(\underbrace{\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{i}^{\ell}\mathbf{W}_{\text{FFN}}^{1}+\mathbf{b}_{\text{FFN}}^{1}}_{\bm{\zeta}_{i}^{\ell}})\mathbf{W}_{\text{FFN}}^{2}+\mathbf{b}_{\text{FFN}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>act</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>FFN</mtext><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐛</mi><mtext>FFN</mtext><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msubsup><mi>𝜻</mi><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>FFN</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐛</mi><mtext>FFN</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=f_{\text{act}}(\underbrace{\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{i}^{\ell}\mathbf{W}_{\text{FFN}}^{1}+\mathbf{b}_{\text{FFN}}^{1}}_{\bm{\zeta}_{i}^{\ell}})\mathbf{W}_{\text{FFN}}^{2}+\mathbf{b}_{\text{FFN}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E8X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z_{\text{FFN},i}^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mtext>FFN</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z_{\text{FFN},i}^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=f_{\text{act}}^{(\zeta_{i}^{\ell})}\left(\sum_{k=1}^{N}\zeta_{i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell}\right)W_{\text{FFN}}^{2}+b_{\text{FFN}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mtext>act</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mtext>FFN</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mtext>FFN</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=f_{\text{act}}^{(\zeta_{i}^{\ell})}\left(\sum_{k=1}^{N}\zeta_{i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell}\right)W_{\text{FFN}}^{2}+b_{\text{FFN}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E8Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{k=1}^{N}\underbrace{\theta(\zeta_{i}^{\ell})\odot\zeta_{i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell}+b_{\text{FFN}}^{2}}_{z_{\text{FFN},i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mtext>FFN</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mtext>FFN</mtext><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></munder></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{k=1}^{N}\underbrace{\theta(\zeta_{i}^{\ell})\odot\zeta_{i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell}+b_{\text{FFN}}^{2}}_{z_{\text{FFN},i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A propagation mechanism is implemented for decomposed token representations across multiple layers, preserving their distinct identities and preventing representation conflation:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E9X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{i}^{\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{i}^{\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\operatorname{LN}\left(\sum_{k=1}^{N}\underbrace{\left[\tilde{z}_{i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell}+z_{\mathrm{FFN},i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell}\right]}_{z_{\mathrm{FFN}+,i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>LN</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>FFN</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>FFN</mi><mo rspace="0em">+</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></munder></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\operatorname{LN}\left(\sum_{k=1}^{N}\underbrace{\left[\tilde{z}_{i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell}+z_{\mathrm{FFN},i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell}\right]}_{z_{\mathrm{FFN}+,i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E9Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{k=1}^{N}\underbrace{gz_{\mathrm{FFN}+,i}^{\ell}\left(z_{\mathrm{FFN}+,i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell}\right)+\beta}_{x_{i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>FFN</mi><mo rspace="0em">+</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>FFN</mi><mo rspace="0em">+</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></munder></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{k=1}^{N}\underbrace{gz_{\mathrm{FFN}+,i}^{\ell}\left(z_{\mathrm{FFN}+,i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell}\right)+\beta}_{x_{i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell+1}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The final classification mechanism utilizes these decomposed vectors to quantitatively ascertain the precise directional impact (positive or negative) exerted by each input token upon specific target classes.
The established formulations within this section permit the derivation of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math> from its antecedent, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i\Leftarrow k}^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>. Through the iterative application of this procedure across the entirety of the layered architecture, the final value, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i\Leftarrow k}^{L+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i\Leftarrow k}^{L+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, is consequently ascertained. For the task-specific classification head,</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E10X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y_{c}=\sum_{k=1}^{N}y_{c\Leftarrow k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y_{c}=\sum_{k=1}^{N}y_{c\Leftarrow k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where the term <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{c\Leftarrow k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{c\Leftarrow k}</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the specific contribution of the <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>th</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> token towards the overall prediction score.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="font-size:120%;">4.  Methodology</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.3. Token Attribution ‣ 2. Related Work ‣ FrugalPrompt: Reducing Contextual Overhead in Large Language Models via Token Attribution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> portrays an overview of our proposed pipeline. We obtain the saliency scores using the token attribution methods discussed in Sec. <a href="#S3" title="3. Preliminaries: Token Attribution ‣ FrugalPrompt: Reducing Contextual Overhead in Large Language Models via Token Attribution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and employ frugalization via token filtration to generate a condensed yet semantically representative version of an input sample. This technique identifies and retains only the most salient tokens, thereby reducing input dimensionality while preserving the core meaning for the given task.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:110%;">4.1.  Ranking via Saliency Scores</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define a task-specific scoring function <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{\tau}\in[\text{GlobEnc,\ DecompX}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>GlobEnc, DecompX</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{\tau}\in[\text{GlobEnc,\ DecompX}]</annotation></semantics></math> for a task <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, which assigns a saliency score to each token in a given text, <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{\tau}:\mathcal{T}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{\tau}:\mathcal{T}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the space of the token sequences and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the length of the sequence. For the input token sequence, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\langle t_{1},t_{2},\dots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\langle t_{1},t_{2},\dots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, the scoring function produces a saliency vector,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}=\varphi_{\tau}(T)=\langle s_{1},s_{2},\dots,s_{n}\rangle,\quad s_{i}\in\mathbb{R}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}=\varphi_{\tau}(T)=\langle s_{1},s_{2},\dots,s_{n}\rangle,\quad s_{i}\in\mathbb{R},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where each score <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> reflects the relative contribution of token <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to the model’s output for the specified task.
Tokens are then ranked in monotone decreasing order of their attribution scores to obtain a permutation, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, formed of the token indices, such that,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\pi(1)}\geq s_{\pi(2)}\geq\cdots\geq s_{\pi(n)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\pi(1)}\geq s_{\pi(2)}\geq\cdots\geq s_{\pi(n)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:110%;">4.2.  Top-<math id="S4.SS2.m1" class="ltx_align_left" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> Filtering</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To construct the <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">frugalized representation</span>, we retain only the top <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=\left\lceil\frac{k}{100}\cdot n\right\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mn>100</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=\left\lceil\frac{k}{100}\cdot n\right\rceil</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is the retention percentage. The selected indices form the sequence,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{S}_{k}=\langle\pi(1),\pi(2),\dots,\pi(p)\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{S}_{k}=\langle\pi(1),\pi(2),\dots,\pi(p)\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We reindex <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in monotone increasing order to produce <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{S}_{k\uparrow}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{S}_{k\uparrow}</annotation></semantics></math>. The frugalized sequence <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is produced by restoring the selected tokens to their original order, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{F}_{k}=\langle t_{i}\lvert i\in{S}_{k\uparrow}\rangle" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{F}_{k}=\langle t_{i}\lvert i\in{S}_{k\uparrow}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">ensuring that the temporal coherence of the sequence is preserved, while uninformative tokens are discarded.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We elucidate our frugalization approach with an example.
For the input sequence <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{T}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{T}=</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘The’’</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘movie’’</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘was’’</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘good’’</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘,’’</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘and’’</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘I’’</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘liked’’</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘it’’</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘very’’</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘much’’<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></span>, suppose the attribution scores for sentiment analysis prioritize content and sentiment words (<span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘movie’’</span>: <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.95</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘good’’</span>: <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.90" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.90</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.90</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘much’’</span>: <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.85" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.85</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.85</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘liked’’</span>: <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.80</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.80</annotation></semantics></math>). Upon filtering the top <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=40\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=40\%</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=4</annotation></semantics></math>) and reordering them according to their original relative order, we get the reduced sequence</p>
<table id="S4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{F}_{40}=\langle\texttt{``movie''},\texttt{``good''},\texttt{``liked''},\texttt{``much''}\rangle" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>40</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">‘‘movie’’</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">‘‘good’’</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">‘‘liked’’</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">‘‘much’’</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{F}_{40}=\langle\texttt{``movie''},\texttt{``good''},\texttt{``liked''},\texttt{``much''}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the original order is preserved to maintain syntactic coherence. The frugalized output <em id="S4.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">“movie good liked much”</em> is thus created, discarding the less salient tokens (<span id="S4.SS2.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘The’’</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘was’’</span>, etc.) while retaining semantic essence.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:455.2pt;height:330.7pt;vertical-align:-163.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-65.9pt,47.9pt) scale(0.775529404669858,0.775529404669858) ;">
<table id="S4.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
