<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Cost comparison of models per 1 million tokens. The Gemini 2.0 Flash Thinking model has output cost fluctuations due to the number of thinking tokens produced.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:110%;">6.2.  Choice of Attribution Method</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following both Tab. <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2. Top-𝑘 Filtering ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ FrugalPrompt: Reducing Contextual Overhead in Large Language Models via Token Attribution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2. Top-𝑘 Filtering ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ FrugalPrompt: Reducing Contextual Overhead in Large Language Models via Token Attribution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, for classification and QA tasks, GlobEnc outperforms DecompX across all metrics. However, in reasoning, DecompX outshines GlobEnc. DecompX’s subword-level attribution that includes linear activations may help retain more important tokens for reasoning, but might overwhelm the models in other tasks. For summarization (Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2. Top-𝑘 Filtering ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ FrugalPrompt: Reducing Contextual Overhead in Large Language Models via Token Attribution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), GlobEnc usually outperforms DecompX, except for the GPT-3.5 model. While DecompX is reported to produce <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">better</span> attribution scores <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Modarressi et al. (2023)</a></cite>, most models find the token sequence filtered by word-level attention, like GlobEnc, to be more useful in retaining performance across most tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:110%;">6.3.  Performance <span id="S6.SS3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_align_left ltx_font_italic">vs.</span> Cost</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The cost per million tokens for each model was taken from the respective provider’s website, at the time of writing, and is reported in Tab. <a href="#S6.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 6.1. Performance across Tasks ‣ 6. Result and Analysis ‣ FrugalPrompt: Reducing Contextual Overhead in Large Language Models via Token Attribution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. The reader should note that the price is subject to change. The cost of the open-source Llama models was estimated using the lowest available rate on OpenRouter<a href="#footnote1" title="footnote 1 ‣ 5.3. Models ‣ 5. Experiment ‣ FrugalPrompt: Reducing Contextual Overhead in Large Language Models via Token Attribution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fig.<a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2. Top-𝑘 Filtering ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ FrugalPrompt: Reducing Contextual Overhead in Large Language Models via Token Attribution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2. Top-𝑘 Filtering ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ FrugalPrompt: Reducing Contextual Overhead in Large Language Models via Token Attribution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> show the performance difference between the reduced set of tokens and 100% tokens for all the models. For sentiment analysis and question answering, we observe that using a reduced set of tokens generally retains the performance and has a softer decline as we go down the cost. However, reducing the tokens sharply declines the performance in reasoning tasks, making it unsuitable for token reduction. The results are mixed for summarization tasks: while most models have a softer performance decline across the metrics, methods such as GPT-3.5 using GlobEnc experience rapid performance degradation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:110%;">6.4.  Performance across Models</h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2. Top-𝑘 Filtering ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ FrugalPrompt: Reducing Contextual Overhead in Large Language Models via Token Attribution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>,<a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2. Top-𝑘 Filtering ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ FrugalPrompt: Reducing Contextual Overhead in Large Language Models via Token Attribution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> illustrate that for smaller models, token attribution offers negligible cost savings relative to the sharper performance degradation it incurs. Consequently, token reduction proves to be more practical for larger, costlier models such as o3-mini. Legacy models such as GPT-3.5 often display abrupt drops in performance, <span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, in summarization using GlobEnc, necessitating greater caution when reducing tokens.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:110%;">6.5.  Random &amp; Bottom <math id="S6.SS5.m1" class="ltx_align_left" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> tokens</h3>

<div id="S6.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We select random <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\%</annotation></semantics></math> and the bottom <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\%</annotation></semantics></math> tokens (based on attribution scores), while preserving the text order, to establish a baseline against our token reduction method.
As shown in <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.5. Evaluation Metrics ‣ 5. Experiment ‣ FrugalPrompt: Reducing Contextual Overhead in Large Language Models via Token Attribution" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 2</span></a>, classification, summarization, and question answering exhibit strong retention in performance across varying thresholds. This strongly suggests potential task contamination <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Flanigan (2024)</a></cite>, as the ground truth answers of the test sets are easily available on the internet. Reasoning tasks experience a sharper decline, indicating genuine reliance on contextual information for solving the task.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="font-size:120%;">7.  Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The surging cost of large language models has often made it inaccessible to people in under-resourced environments, while increasing carbon footprint and inference time latency. Our work aims to bridge this gap by introducing prompt compression at a token-attribution level, which can reduce the cost of LLM inference and make it accessible to a wider range of users. We also reveal potential instances of task contamination that should be considered while evaluating models. We wish to explore adaptive frugalization strategies that dynamically adjust token retention based on task complexity and model confidence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As our token reduction pipeline heavily relies on attribution methods, misalignment between salience and true causal importance could produce suboptimal performance. The task specificity of the attribution methods also makes them unsuitable for certain tasks, such as reasoning, and evaluation in multi-task settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Ethical Consideration</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As identified in our work, performance retention in certain tasks might stem from the model’s exposure to benchmark data during pretraining. This raises concerns about the validity of LLM evaluation and the need for contamination-free datasets. Prompt compression can also potentially remove safety-critical tokens and hence should be used with caution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Bibliographical References</h2>

</section>
<section id="bib" class="ltx_bibliography">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_bibliography"></h2>

<ul class="ltx_biblist">
      
<li id="bib.bib1" class="ltx_bibitem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem">Abnar and Zuidema (2020)</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">
